**Proof.**

Work modulo 2. For each $j$, consider the vector $v_j = (a_j, b_j, c_j) \in \mathbb{F}_2^3$. Since at least one of $a_j, b_j, c_j$ is odd, $v_j \neq (0,0,0)$, so $v_j$ is one of the $7$ nonzero vectors of $\mathbb{F}_2^3$.

For integers $r, s, t$, the parity of $ra_j + sb_j + tc_j$ equals
$$
(r,s,t)\cdot v_j \pmod{2},
$$
which depends only on $w := (r,s,t) \bmod 2 \in \mathbb{F}_2^3$. Thus $ra_j+sb_j+tc_j$ is odd exactly when $w \cdot v_j = 1$.

There are $8$ choices for $w$. For any fixed nonzero $v_j$, the map $w \mapsto w \cdot v_j$ is a nonzero linear functional on $\mathbb{F}_2^3$, so its kernel has size $4$ and exactly $4$ of the $8$ vectors $w$ satisfy $w \cdot v_j = 1$. Among these $4$, note that $w = (0,0,0)$ gives $w \cdot v_j = 0$, so all $4$ vectors with $w \cdot v_j = 1$ are **nonzero**. Equivalently, among the $7$ nonzero vectors $w$, exactly $4$ satisfy $w \cdot v_j = 1$.

Now sum over all $j$ and all $7$ nonzero $w$:
$$
\sum_{w \neq 0}\;\sum_{j=1}^{N} \mathbf{1}[w \cdot v_j = 1] \;=\; \sum_{j=1}^{N}\;\sum_{w \neq 0} \mathbf{1}[w \cdot v_j = 1] \;=\; \sum_{j=1}^{N} 4 \;=\; 4N.
$$
By the pigeonhole (averaging) principle, there exists a nonzero $w^* = (r_0, s_0, t_0) \in \mathbb{F}_2^3$ with
$$
\sum_{j=1}^{N} \mathbf{1}[w^* \cdot v_j = 1] \;\geq\; \frac{4N}{7}.
$$
Choosing any integers $r, s, t$ with $(r,s,t) \equiv (r_0, s_0, t_0) \pmod{2}$ (e.g., $r=r_0, s=s_0, t=t_0 \in \{0,1\}$), we have $ra_j + sb_j + tc_j$ odd for at least $4N/7$ values of $j$. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
